    "link": "https://idrc-crdi.ca/en/funding/call-concept-notes-socio-economic-impacts-artificial-intelligence-africa",
    "objective": "Call for concept notes: Socio-economic impacts of artificial intelligence in Africa | IDRC - International Development Research Centre\nSkip to main content\nTo homepage\nTo homepage\nEnglish\nFrançais\nEspañol\nToggle Menu\nToggle Menu\nFulltext search\nMobile Main Navigation\nWhat we do\nWhere we work\nHow we work\nProjects we support\nClimate resilient food systems\nGlobal health\nEducation and science\nDemocratic and inclusive governance\nSustainable inclusive economies\nFunding\nGrants and awards\nApplying for funding\nManaging funds\nResources for IDRC grantees\nPartnerships\nAbout IDRC\nAbout\nCareers\nAnnual revie",
    "description": "Call for concept notes: Socio-economic impacts of artificial intelligence in Africa | IDRC - International Development Research Centre\nSkip to main content\nTo homepage\nTo homepage\nEnglish\nFrançais\nEspañol\nToggle Menu\nToggle Menu\nFulltext search\nMobile Main Navigation\nWhat we do\nWhere we work\nHow we work\nProjects we support\nClimate resilient food systems\nGlobal health\nEducation and science\nDemocratic and inclusive governance\nSustainable inclusive economies\nFunding\nGrants and awards\nApplying for funding\nManaging funds\nResources for IDRC grantees\nPartnerships\nAbout IDRC\nAbout\nCareers\nAnnual review\nTransparency\nLeadership\nPolicies and commitments\nNews and events\nNews\nEvents\nPerspectives\nResearch in Action\nStories\nMedia\nTo homepage\nEnglish\nFrançais\nEspañol\nToggle Menu\nFooter\nCareers\nContact Us\nDigital library\nSub Footer Menu\nAccessibility\nEquality Statement\nOpen access policy\nPrivacy Notice\nResearch ethics\nTerms of use\nTransparency\nMain navigation\nWhat we do\nWhere we work\nHow we work\nProjects we support\nClimate resilient food systems\nGlobal health\nEducation and science\nDemocratic and inclusive governance\nSustainable inclusive economies\nFunding\nGrants and awards\nApplying for funding\nManaging funds\nResources for IDRC grantees\nPartnerships\nAbout IDRC\nAbout\nCareers\nAnnual review\nTransparency\nLeadership\nPolicies and commitments\nNews and events\nNews\nEvents\nPerspectives\nResearch in Action\nStories\nMedia\nFulltext search\nHome\nFunding\nCall for concept notes: Socio-economic impacts of artificial intelligence in Africa\nClosed\nCall for\nConcept notes\nDeadline\nWednesday, September 17, 2025 - 23:59 ET\nPrograms\nEducation and Science\nDuration\nUp to 36 months\nTopic(s)\nDevelopment\nEconomics\nGovernance\nSocial Policy\nFunded by\nIDRC, FCDO\nBudget\nUp to CAD1,000,000 per project\nPoint of contact\nai4dafrica@idrc.ca\nType\nGrant\nStatus\nClosed\nScope\nThe International Development Research Centre (IDRC) and the United Kingdom’s Foreign, Commonwealth and Development Office (FCDO), as part of the Artificial Intelligence for Development (AI4D) program, are pleased to announce a call for concept notes on the socio-economic impacts of artificial intelligence (AI) in Africa.\nThis call supports research that explores how AI is reshaping labour markets, productivity, and inequality across Africa, supporting policy makers to ensure AI works for everyone, especially the most vulnerable.\nWe invite concept notes that explore the socio-economic impacts of AI in African LMICs, with a focus on differential impacts, inequality and marginalized populations. Projects should address key knowledge gaps and generate evidence to guide responsible AI policies.\nResponsible AI is safe, inclusive, rights-based, ethical, and sustainable AI.\nResearch should align with one or more of the following themes:\nLabour and Livelihoods: How AI is transforming labour markets and employment, especially for youth and informal workers.\nProductivity and Economic Transformation: The role of AI in reshaping sectors and supporting inclusive growth.\nPoverty and Inequality: Risks of exclusion and unequal benefit distribution across demographic groups.\nGlobal Inequality and AI Colonialism: Power imbalances in AI development and strategies for local innovation.\nThrough this call, AI4D will award up to four grants for up to CAD1 million per project. Projects must be completed within 36 months, including all research and reporting activities. Only short-listed teams will be asked to submit full research proposals for review and funding consideration. Selected projects are anticipated to begin on January 1, 2026.\nEligibility\nApplicants must be legally registered organizations based in African low- and middle-income countries (LMICs), with relevant expertise and institutional capacity. Proposals and team expertise must align with the call’s themes. Teams need to meet the eligibility criteria outlined in the call.\nMore details\nFor more information, please consult the detailed\nCall for Concept Notes\n.\nIf you have any q",
